var e={html:[{question:"What is the difference between `async` and `defer` on a script tag?",answer:'Both download the script in parallel without blocking HTML parsing. `async` runs as soon as the file arrives, so execution order is not guaranteed and it can interrupt parsing. `defer` runs after parsing finishes, before `DOMContentLoaded`, in document order. Module scripts (`type="module"`) are deferred by default.'},{question:`Explain the critical rendering path and how markup choices affect it.`,answer:"The browser builds the DOM from HTML and the CSSOM from CSS, combines them into a render tree, then runs layout, paint and composite. Stylesheets are render-blocking and classic synchronous scripts are parser-blocking. Shorten the path by inlining critical CSS, using `defer`, adding `media` attributes to non-critical stylesheets and preloading the LCP resource."},{question:`When should you use ARIA, and what is the first rule of ARIA?`,answer:'Prefer a native element with built-in semantics (`button`, `nav`, `dialog`) over ARIA. ARIA only changes the accessibility tree, not behaviour, so `<div role="button">` still needs `tabindex="0"` plus Enter and Space key handling. Use ARIA when no native element can express the role, state or relationship.'},{question:"How do `preload`, `prefetch` and `preconnect` differ?",answer:"`preload` fetches a resource the current page needs with high priority and requires an `as` value. `prefetch` fetches a resource at low priority for a likely future navigation. `preconnect` completes DNS, TCP and TLS early for an origin without downloading anything. Overusing `preload` competes with critical requests."},{question:'How do `srcset`, `sizes` and `loading="lazy"` improve image performance?',answer:'`srcset` lists image candidates by width or pixel density, and `sizes` tells the browser the rendered width so it can choose the smallest suitable file. `loading="lazy"` defers off-screen images. Always set `width` and `height` to prevent layout shift, and never lazy-load the LCP image.'}],css:[{question:"How does the cascade resolve conflicts when using `@layer`?",answer:"Order is origin and importance, then cascade layers, then specificity, then source order. Unlayered styles beat layered styles, and later layers beat earlier ones. For `!important` declarations the layer order is reversed. Specificity is counted as (IDs, classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, types); `:where()` adds zero, while `:is()`, `:not()` and `:has()` take their most specific argument."},{question:"What creates a stacking context, and why can `z-index: 9999` fail?",answer:"Positioned elements with a non-auto `z-index`, `position: fixed` or `sticky`, `opacity` below 1, `transform`, `filter`, `isolation: isolate`, and flex or grid items with `z-index` all create one. `z-index` only compares siblings inside the same stacking context, so a child can never escape a parent context with a lower layer."},{question:`Which properties are cheap to animate and why?`,answer:"`transform` and `opacity` can usually be handled by the compositor thread without layout or paint. Animating `width`, `top` or `margin` triggers layout; `color`, `background` and `box-shadow` trigger paint. Use `will-change` sparingly because each promoted layer costs GPU memory."},{question:`When would you choose container queries over media queries?`,answer:"Media queries respond to the viewport; container queries respond to the size of an ancestor declared with `container-type: inline-size`. That makes a component adapt to where it is placed, such as a sidebar versus a main column, so it is truly reusable. Container units like `cqi` size content relative to the container."},{question:`What is a block formatting context (BFC) and how do you create one?`,answer:"A BFC is an isolated layout region: it contains its floats, and its margins do not collapse with those outside it. `display: flow-root` is the side-effect-free way to create one. Floats, absolute positioning, `overflow` other than `visible` or `clip`, `inline-block`, and flex or grid items also establish one."}],javascript:[{question:`Explain the event loop, tasks and microtasks.`,answer:"Synchronous code runs on the call stack. When a task finishes, the microtask queue (promise reactions, `queueMicrotask`, `MutationObserver`) is drained completely before rendering and the next task (`setTimeout`, I/O, UI events). So `console.log` runs first, then `Promise.then`, then `setTimeout(fn, 0)`. A long microtask chain can starve rendering."},{question:`How can closures cause memory leaks, and how do you prevent them?`,answer:"A closure keeps its lexical environment alive. Leaks happen when long-lived references, such as event listeners, timers, global caches or subscriptions, hold closures that capture large objects or detached DOM nodes. Remove listeners (an `AbortController` signal helps), clear timers, and use `WeakMap` or `WeakRef` for caches keyed by objects."},{question:"What rules determine the value of `this`?",answer:"In priority order: `new` binding, explicit binding with `call`, `apply` or `bind`, implicit binding from `obj.method()`, then default binding, which is `undefined` in strict mode. Arrow functions have no own `this`; they capture it lexically and ignore `call` and `bind`. Passing `obj.method` as a callback loses the implicit binding."},{question:"Compare `Promise.all`, `allSettled`, `race` and `any`.",answer:"`all` fulfils with every value or rejects on the first rejection. `allSettled` waits for everything and returns each status. `race` settles with the first promise to settle, fulfilled or rejected. `any` fulfils with the first fulfilment and rejects with an `AggregateError` only if all reject."},{question:`How do classes relate to prototypal inheritance?`,answer:"Objects delegate property lookups through the `[[Prototype]]` chain. `class` is mostly syntax over constructor functions and prototypes, but it also enforces strict mode, makes methods non-enumerable, requires `new`, has a temporal dead zone, and supports truly private `#fields` that cannot be reached by reflection."}],typescript:[{question:"What is the difference between `any`, `unknown` and `never`?",answer:"`any` switches off type checking. `unknown` is the safe top type: it accepts any value but must be narrowed before use. `never` is the bottom type with no values; assigning a value to `never` in a `default` branch gives compile-time exhaustiveness checking for unions."},{question:"How do conditional types and `infer` work?",answer:"A conditional type chooses a branch based on assignability, for example `T extends (infer U)[] ? U : T`, where `infer U` captures the element type. Conditional types distribute over unions when `T` is a naked type parameter; wrap it as `[T] extends [X]` to prevent distribution."},{question:`TypeScript is structurally typed. How do you get nominal-like safety?`,answer:'Compatibility is based on shape, not name, so two types with the same members are interchangeable. Excess-property checks only apply to fresh object literals. To stop mixing values such as `UserId` and `OrderId`, use branded types like `string & { readonly __brand: "UserId" }` created through a validating factory.'},{question:"What problem does the `satisfies` operator solve?",answer:"`satisfies` checks that a value conforms to a type without widening the value’s inferred type. Unlike an annotation, `const routes = { ... } satisfies Record<string, Route>` keeps exact keys and literal types for autocomplete and narrowing while still reporting mistakes."},{question:`How do mapped types with key remapping work?`,answer:"Mapped types iterate keys with `[K in keyof T]`, and an `as` clause renames them, for example combining a template literal type with `Capitalize<K>` to turn `name` into `getName`. Modifiers like `-readonly` and `-?` remove flags. Built-in utilities such as `Partial`, `Required`, `Pick` and `Readonly` are built from mapped types."}],angular:[{question:"How does `OnPush` change detection decide when to check a component?",answer:"An `OnPush` component is checked when an input reference changes, an event fires in its template, an `async` pipe emits, a signal read in the template changes, or `markForCheck()` is called. Mutating an object in place does not trigger it. Combined with signals, Angular can run zoneless change detection."},{question:`When do you use signals versus RxJS?`,answer:"Signals (`signal`, `computed`, `effect`) are synchronous values with automatic dependency tracking, ideal for component and UI state. RxJS suits asynchronous event streams, cancellation and time-based operators such as debouncing and retries. Bridge them with `toSignal` and `toObservable` from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop`."},{question:`Explain Angular’s injector hierarchy.`,answer:'Environment injectors (platform, root and lazy-loaded routes) hold services such as those with `providedIn: "root"`, which are also tree-shakable. Element injectors come from `providers` on components and directives. Resolution walks up the element tree, then the environment tree, and can be adjusted with `self`, `skipSelf`, `host` and `optional` options on `inject()`.'},{question:`What are deferrable views and when would you use them?`,answer:"`@defer` lazily loads standalone components, directives and pipes in a block, splitting them into separate chunks. Triggers include `on viewport`, `on idle`, `on interaction`, `on hover`, `on timer` and `when condition`, with `@placeholder`, `@loading` and `@error` blocks. Use it for heavy below-the-fold UI such as charts or editors."},{question:`How do you avoid subscription leaks in Angular?`,answer:"Prefer the `async` pipe or `toSignal`, which unsubscribe automatically. For manual subscriptions use `takeUntilDestroyed()` with `DestroyRef`. Avoid nested `subscribe` calls; compose streams with higher-order operators such as `switchMap`, which also cancels outdated HTTP requests."}],rxjs:[{question:"Compare `switchMap`, `mergeMap`, `concatMap` and `exhaustMap`.",answer:"`switchMap` cancels the previous inner stream, ideal for typeahead search. `mergeMap` runs inner streams concurrently, for parallel requests. `concatMap` queues them in order, for sequential saves. `exhaustMap` ignores new values while one is active, for preventing double login submits."},{question:`What is the difference between hot and cold observables, and how do you share one?`,answer:"A cold observable creates a new producer for each subscriber, so each subscription to an HTTP call sends a request. A hot observable shares one producer, such as DOM events or a `Subject`. Use `share()` or `shareReplay({ bufferSize: 1, refCount: true })`; without `refCount: true` the source can stay subscribed and leak."},{question:"When do you use `Subject`, `BehaviorSubject`, `ReplaySubject` and `AsyncSubject`?",answer:"`Subject` multicasts with no stored value. `BehaviorSubject` requires an initial value and gives new subscribers the current value, making it suitable for state. `ReplaySubject` replays a configurable buffer of past values. `AsyncSubject` emits only the last value when the source completes."},{question:`How should errors be handled so a stream keeps working?`,answer:"An error terminates the observable. Put `catchError` inside the inner observable of `switchMap` so a failed request returns a fallback while the outer stream, such as search input, stays alive. Use `retry({ count, delay })` for transient failures with back-off."},{question:"Explain `debounceTime`, `throttleTime` and `auditTime`.",answer:"`debounceTime` emits only after the source has been silent for the duration, ideal for search input. `throttleTime` emits the first value and ignores others during the window, useful for scroll handlers. `auditTime` waits for the window and emits the latest value, useful for resize events."}],scss:[{question:"Why should you use `@use` and `@forward` instead of `@import`?",answer:"`@import` is deprecated in Dart Sass. `@use` loads each module once, namespaces its members, keeps members beginning with `-` or `_` private, and allows configuration with `with (...)`. `@forward` re-exports a module’s members, which is how you build a public entry point for a design system."},{question:"When do you use a mixin instead of `@extend`?",answer:"Mixins copy declarations and accept arguments, so output is predictable. `@extend` merges selectors, cannot extend across media queries, and can produce selector bloat or unexpected ordering. Prefer mixins for reusable patterns and reserve `@extend` for genuinely related selectors."},{question:`Compare Sass variables with CSS custom properties.`,answer:`Sass variables are resolved at compile time and disappear from output. CSS custom properties exist at runtime, participate in the cascade and inheritance, and can change via media queries, themes or JavaScript. Use Sass for build-time logic and CSS custom properties for theming such as dark mode.`},{question:`How do you generate utility classes from a design-token map?`,answer:'Store tokens in a map such as `$spacing: (sm: 0.5rem, md: 1rem)` and loop with `@each $name, $value in $spacing` to emit classes. Use `map.get` from `@use "sass:map"`. For division use `math.div` from `sass:math`, because `/` as division is deprecated.'}],tablets:[{question:`How do you design for devices that may or may not support hover?`,answer:"Use interaction media features such as `@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)` to add hover enhancements only where they work. Never hide essential actions behind hover. Make touch targets comfortable: WCAG 2.2 requires at least 24×24 CSS pixels, and 44–48 pixels is the common platform guidance."},{question:`Why is user-agent sniffing unreliable for tablets?`,answer:"Since iPadOS 13, Safari on iPad requests desktop sites and reports a macOS-like user agent. Prefer feature detection, for example `navigator.maxTouchPoints > 1`, interaction media queries and content-driven breakpoints rather than device-specific widths."},{question:`How should a layout respond to orientation changes and split view?`,answer:'Split-screen multitasking means the viewport can be almost any width, so build fluid layouts with `minmax()`, `auto-fit` and `clamp()`. Use container queries for components. If JavaScript must react, listen to `matchMedia("(orientation: portrait)")` changes rather than the deprecated `orientationchange` event.'},{question:`How do you make a data table usable on a tablet?`,answer:"Keep key columns visible with `position: sticky`, allow horizontal scrolling inside a labelled, focusable region, and progressively hide or collapse lower-priority columns with container queries. Preserve semantic table markup so screen readers can still announce headers."}],mobiles:[{question:"Why can `100vh` be wrong on mobile, and what is the fix?",answer:"Mobile browser toolbars expand and collapse, so `100vh` can be taller than the visible area. Use `svh` for the smallest viewport, `lvh` for the largest, or `dvh` for the dynamic viewport. Combine with `width=device-width, initial-scale=1` in the viewport meta tag."},{question:`How do you support notches and rounded corners?`,answer:"Add `viewport-fit=cover` to the viewport meta tag, then pad fixed headers, footers and edge content using `env(safe-area-inset-top)` and the other inset values, typically inside `max()` so a minimum padding is kept."},{question:`What are the Core Web Vitals thresholds and how do you improve them on mobile?`,answer:'Good scores at the 75th percentile are LCP ≤ 2.5 s, INP ≤ 200 ms and CLS ≤ 0.1. Mobile CPUs are slower, so reduce and split JavaScript, break up long tasks, prioritise the LCP image with `fetchpriority="high"`, and reserve space for images, ads and embeds.'},{question:`How do you improve mobile form input?`,answer:"Use the correct `type`, `inputmode` and `autocomplete` values so users get the right keyboard and autofill. Keep input font size at 16 px or larger to avoid iOS zooming on focus, and show errors next to fields without relying on colour alone."},{question:`Why use passive listeners and pointer events?`,answer:"A non-passive `touchstart` or `touchmove` listener can block scrolling while the browser waits to see if `preventDefault()` is called; `{ passive: true }` removes that wait. Pointer events unify mouse, touch and pen handling, and `touch-action` declares which gestures the browser should handle."}],desktop:[{question:`How do you make complex widgets keyboard accessible?`,answer:"Keep a logical tab order and a visible `:focus-visible` style. Use a roving `tabindex` with arrow keys inside composite widgets such as tabs, menus and grids. A native `<dialog>` opened with `showModal()` makes the background inert and supports Escape to close; restore focus to the trigger afterwards."},{question:`How do you design layouts for very wide screens?`,answer:"Limit text line length to roughly 45–75 characters with a `max-width` in `ch`, use responsive grids with `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(...))`, and scale typography fluidly with `clamp()`. Use extra width for multi-column content rather than stretching single columns."},{question:`What does WCAG reflow require at 400% zoom?`,answer:`Content must reflow without two-dimensional scrolling at a width equivalent to 320 CSS pixels, which is a 1280 px window at 400% zoom. Use relative units, avoid fixed-width containers and test with browser zoom, not only device emulation. Data tables and maps are the main exceptions.`},{question:`How do you keep large data views fast?`,answer:"Virtualise long lists with a tool such as Angular CDK virtual scrolling so only visible rows are in the DOM. Use `content-visibility: auto` with `contain-intrinsic-size` for off-screen sections, debounce expensive filters and move heavy computation to a Web Worker."}],chrome:[{question:`Describe Chrome’s multi-process architecture and why it matters.`,answer:"Chrome separates the browser process, renderer processes isolated per site, the GPU process and the network service. Site Isolation protects against attacks like Spectre. Within a renderer, the compositor thread can keep `transform` and `opacity` animations smooth even when the main thread is busy."},{question:`How do you find and fix a janky interaction in the Performance panel?`,answer:"Record the interaction, look for long tasks over 50 ms and inspect the flame chart. Purple layout bars after JavaScript often indicate forced synchronous layout, where code reads `offsetHeight` after writing styles. Batch reads before writes, and split long work with `scheduler.yield()` where supported."},{question:`How do you diagnose a memory leak with DevTools?`,answer:`Take a heap snapshot, repeat the suspected action several times, take another and compare them. Filter for detached DOM nodes and inspect retainers to see what keeps objects alive. The allocation timeline shows objects that are allocated and never collected.`},{question:`What is the difference between Lighthouse scores and field data?`,answer:"Lighthouse is lab data from a single simulated device and network, useful for debugging. Field data from the Chrome UX Report or real-user monitoring with the `web-vitals` library reflects actual users, and the 75th percentile is what Core Web Vitals assessments use."}],firefox:[{question:`How does Firefox’s engine differ from Chrome’s?`,answer:`Firefox uses Gecko with Quantum components, including the Stylo parallel CSS engine written in Rust and the WebRender GPU renderer, and SpiderMonkey for JavaScript. Chrome uses Blink and V8. Different engines expose different bugs and feature timelines, so cross-engine testing matters.`},{question:`Which Firefox DevTools features help most with layout debugging?`,answer:`The Grid and Flexbox inspectors overlay tracks, gaps and line numbers, the Inactive CSS hints explain why a declaration has no effect, and the Accessibility Inspector audits contrast, keyboard access and the accessibility tree.`},{question:`How does Total Cookie Protection affect embedded apps?`,answer:`Firefox partitions third-party cookies and storage by top-level site, so an iframe from the same provider gets separate storage on each site. Embedded login or chat widgets may lose state. Use the Storage Access API to request unpartitioned access after user interaction.`},{question:`How do you ship a feature safely when browser support differs?`,answer:"Use feature detection with `@supports` in CSS and `CSS.supports()` or API checks in JavaScript, not user-agent sniffing. Check Baseline status before relying on a feature, and provide a working fallback so unsupported browsers get a simpler experience."}],safari:[{question:`Why do Safari bugs often affect other browsers on iOS?`,answer:`Apple has historically required all iOS browsers to use WebKit, so Chrome and Firefox on iOS share Safari’s rendering behaviour. Alternative engines are allowed only in limited regions, such as the EU, so testing WebKit remains essential for mobile users.`},{question:`What is Intelligent Tracking Prevention and how can it break apps?`,answer:"ITP blocks third-party cookies and limits storage that trackers could use, and can cap the lifetime of cookies set by script. Cross-site SSO and embedded widgets may lose sessions. Prefer first-party, server-set `HttpOnly` cookies and the Storage Access API for embedded content."},{question:`Name common Safari layout and input issues.`,answer:"Dynamic toolbars make `100vh` unreliable (use `dvh`), the virtual keyboard can disturb `position: fixed`, inputs under 16 px trigger zoom on focus, and older versions need `-webkit-` prefixes for features such as `backdrop-filter`. Always test on a real device."},{question:`What should you know about PWAs on iOS?`,answer:"Web push is available for web apps added to the Home Screen since iOS 16.4. There is no `beforeinstallprompt` event, so you must guide users to Add to Home Screen. Storage can be evicted under pressure, so treat offline data as a cache and sync important data to the server."}],edge:[{question:`How is modern Edge different from legacy Edge?`,answer:`Since 2020 Edge is built on Chromium, using Blink and V8, so rendering largely matches Chrome. Legacy EdgeHTML behaviour is gone. Edge can still open legacy enterprise sites in IE mode through an enterprise site list, which can matter for intranet applications.`},{question:`What should you test specifically in Edge?`,answer:"Test enterprise policies, IE mode routing, default form-control and scrollbar appearance, authentication with Microsoft Entra ID single sign-on, and installed PWA behaviour. Automated tests can run Edge through Playwright using the `msedge` channel."},{question:`How do sleeping tabs affect a web app, and how should you handle it?`,answer:"Edge can put background tabs to sleep, pausing timers and connections. Handle the Page Lifecycle `freeze` and `resume` events and `visibilitychange` to save state, close or reconnect WebSockets, and refresh stale data when the page becomes visible."},{question:`How do enterprise policies affect a front-end deployment?`,answer:`Administrators can control extensions, cookies, pop-ups, downloads and security zones through group policy. Your app should degrade gracefully when storage, extensions or third-party cookies are restricted, and document any policy it depends on for IT teams.`}]};export{e as interviewQuestions};